WEBVTT


00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.700
Downloaded @ subs4free.club

﻿1

00:01:51.182 --> 00:01:52.642
<i>It does now seem like</i>


00:01:52.726 --> 00:01:55.437
that sometime
in the last couple of days


00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:57.647
there's been perhaps
the worst accident


00:01:57.731 --> 00:01:58.773
in the short history


00:01:58.857 --> 00:02:00.692
of the world's nuclear
power industry.


00:02:00.775 --> 00:02:03.087
<i>In Moscow,
Western diplomats have spent the day</i>


00:02:03.111 --> 00:02:04.738
<i>trying to get more information</i>


00:02:04.821 --> 00:02:07.407
<i>about the accident
with no success.</i>


00:02:28.636 --> 00:02:31.765
<i>A zone with a
19-mile radius around Chernobyl</i>


00:02:31.848 --> 00:02:34.476
<i>remains a poisoned
no man's land.</i>


00:03:02.087 --> 00:03:04.923
Soviet television tonight
accused the western media


00:03:05.006 --> 00:03:06.966
of spreading
slanderous inventions


00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:08.551
about the Chernobyl accident.


00:34:33.768 --> 00:34:36.229
<i>It's now
clear that the Soviet Union</i>


00:34:36.312 --> 00:34:38.481
<i>has suffered one of
the worst disasters</i>


00:34:38.564 --> 00:34:40.650
<i>in the history of nuclear power.</i>


00:34:40.733 --> 00:34:42.652
Massive quantities of radiation


00:34:42.735 --> 00:34:44.904
have apparently been
released in an accident


00:34:44.988 --> 00:34:47.740
at the Chernobyl Power
Station in the Ukraine.


00:34:47.824 --> 00:34:49.575
The Soviet version is this,


00:34:49.659 --> 00:34:51.411
one of the atomic reactors


00:34:51.494 --> 00:34:53.204
at the Chernobyl
Atomic Power Plant


00:34:53.287 --> 00:34:55.081
in the city of Kyiv was damaged


00:34:55.164 --> 00:34:57.166
and there is
speculation in Moscow


00:34:57.250 --> 00:34:59.669
that people were injured
and may have died.


00:34:59.752 --> 00:35:01.546
The Soviets may
have been fairly quick


00:35:01.629 --> 00:35:03.047
to acknowledge the accident


00:35:03.131 --> 00:35:06.718
because evidence in the form
of mild and nuclear radiation


00:35:06.801 --> 00:35:09.387
have already reached
beyond the Soviet borders


00:35:09.470 --> 00:35:10.346
to Scandinavia.


00:35:10.430 --> 00:35:11.657
<i>There are indications</i>


00:35:11.681 --> 00:35:13.057
<i>the accident could have happened</i>


00:35:13.141 --> 00:35:13.933
<i>several days ago.</i>


00:35:14.017 --> 00:35:15.018
<i>It would take that long</i>


00:35:15.101 --> 00:35:17.311
<i>for radiation to
drift to Scandinavia</i>


00:35:17.395 --> 00:35:19.063
<i>and to make the
political decision</i>


00:35:19.147 --> 00:35:20.023
<i>to announce it.</i>


00:35:20.106 --> 00:35:20.940
You can be sure


00:35:21.024 --> 00:35:23.151
that the decision
to, to disclose


00:35:23.234 --> 00:35:25.611
was made at the
very highest level,


00:35:25.695 --> 00:35:27.780
undoubtedly by
Gorbachev himself.


00:35:27.864 --> 00:35:29.258
<i>The Soviet press is paying</i>


00:35:29.282 --> 00:35:31.325
<i>signally little attention
to the disaster.</i>


00:35:31.409 --> 00:35:32.744
<i>Newspapers carry only</i>


00:35:32.827 --> 00:35:34.328
<i>the brief official announcement</i>


00:35:34.412 --> 00:35:36.039
<i>inconspicuously placed.</i>


00:35:36.122 --> 00:35:37.642
<i>People have noticed
the small paragraph</i>


00:35:37.707 --> 00:35:39.917
<i>but seemed unaware
of the potential dangers.</i>


00:35:40.001 --> 00:35:42.462
<i>People in
the cities, towns, and villages</i>


00:35:42.545 --> 00:35:43.421
<i>of the Ukraine</i>


00:35:43.504 --> 00:35:45.089
<i>just haven't been told</i>


00:35:45.173 --> 00:35:47.550
<i>what dangers they may face.</i>


00:41:28.474 --> 00:41:31.477
On the eve of the Seven
Nation Summit in Tokyo


00:41:31.560 --> 00:41:33.437
both Mrs. Thatcher
and President Reagan


00:41:33.521 --> 00:41:36.399
have urged the Russians to
say more about Chernobyl.


00:41:36.482 --> 00:41:38.609
<i>Meeting
with Italy's Premier Craxi,</i>


00:41:38.692 --> 00:41:41.779
<i>Mr. Reagan was demanding
answers from the Russians.</i>


00:41:41.862 --> 00:41:45.366
Come forward and tell
everybody exactly what happened.


00:41:45.449 --> 00:41:50.246
You can hardly call this kind
of an accident an internal affair


00:41:50.329 --> 00:41:52.832
when other countries
are threatened


00:41:52.915 --> 00:41:55.459
with the fallout of ra...


00:41:55.543 --> 00:41:57.420
dangerous radioactivity.


00:41:57.503 --> 00:41:58.543
A report in this morning's


00:41:58.587 --> 00:42:00.631
Communist party
newspaper "Pravda"


00:42:00.714 --> 00:42:02.800
indicates that the fire
at the power station


00:42:02.883 --> 00:42:04.510
still may not have been put out


00:42:04.593 --> 00:42:06.846
ten days after it started.


00:42:06.929 --> 00:42:08.806
The newspaper says
there was an explosion


00:42:08.889 --> 00:42:11.350
followed by two
fires at the plant.


00:42:11.434 --> 00:42:13.662
<i>The "Pravda" pays
tribute to the firemen whose efforts</i>


00:42:13.686 --> 00:42:16.439
<i>it says limited the scale of
the damage considerably.</i>


00:42:16.522 --> 00:42:18.691
<i>"Brave bold men" it calls them,</i>


00:42:18.774 --> 00:42:19.900
<i>fighting courageously,</i>


00:42:19.984 --> 00:42:22.278
<i>hardly able to breathe
for soot and smoke,</i>


00:42:22.361 --> 00:42:24.196
<i>their boots sticking
in the asphalt</i>


00:42:24.280 --> 00:42:26.115
<i>melted by the high temperatures.</i>


00:46:07.252 --> 00:46:10.714
<i>As an
American I'm very privileged</i>


00:46:10.798 --> 00:46:13.050
<i>to be able to represent</i>


00:46:13.133 --> 00:46:16.887
<i>in the Soviet Union the
concern of Americans.</i>


00:46:25.604 --> 00:46:27.272
We have agreed


00:46:27.356 --> 00:46:31.985
to a joint Soviet-American
effort in this regard.


00:53:47.254 --> 00:53:50.173
<i>Until the accident,
there were 50 nuclear reactors</i>


00:53:50.257 --> 00:53:51.883
<i>operating in the Soviet Union</i>


00:53:51.967 --> 00:53:54.803
<i>generating 11% of
that nation's electricity.</i>


00:53:54.886 --> 00:53:57.097
<i>Most of the larger Soviet plants</i>


00:53:57.180 --> 00:53:59.432
<i>differ in design
from US reactors.</i>


00:53:59.516 --> 00:54:01.017
<i>The major difference is</i>


00:54:01.101 --> 00:54:03.603
<i>most Soviet nuclear
plants do not have</i>


00:54:03.687 --> 00:54:05.021
<i>containment buildings,</i>


00:54:05.105 --> 00:54:06.815
<i>the thick concrete
dome structures</i>


00:54:06.898 --> 00:54:10.527
<i>Americans are used to
seeing built around US reactors.</i>


00:54:10.610 --> 00:54:13.238
<i>When the worst American
nuclear accident occurred</i>


00:54:13.321 --> 00:54:14.364
<i>at Three Mile Island,</i>


00:54:14.447 --> 00:54:16.324
<i>most leaking radiation
was kept inside</i>


00:54:16.408 --> 00:54:17.742
<i>the containment building.</i>


00:54:17.826 --> 00:54:21.413
<i>If as seems to be the case
there was no containment</i>


00:54:21.496 --> 00:54:23.623
around the reactor
as is the general...


00:54:23.707 --> 00:54:25.017
Can we be
sure of that? What do you...


00:54:25.041 --> 00:54:26.376
I do not, I do not know.


00:54:26.459 --> 00:54:28.020
Do you think
there's a containment building?


00:54:28.044 --> 00:54:30.213
I don't think that there
is a containment building


00:54:30.297 --> 00:54:35.969
because the policy of
Soviet atomic constructors


00:54:36.052 --> 00:54:38.346
was that the reactor's
absolutely safe


00:54:38.430 --> 00:54:41.683
and so there is no need to
have this kind of precaution


00:54:41.766 --> 00:54:44.561
because it indicate
possibility of disaster


00:54:44.644 --> 00:54:47.189
which Russian
always con-considered,


00:54:47.272 --> 00:54:49.691
uh, absolutely impossible.


00:54:49.774 --> 00:54:51.502
So going to be clear about
this, you're, you're going


00:54:51.526 --> 00:54:55.030
as far as to say you think it
almost certain or just probable


00:54:55.113 --> 00:54:58.783
there was no protective
shield around this reactor


00:54:58.867 --> 00:55:00.410
which appears to
have melted down?


00:55:00.493 --> 00:55:02.621
No, I think that
there is no protection,


00:55:02.704 --> 00:55:04.706
protection shield
around this reactor's


00:55:04.789 --> 00:55:06.208
certainly the case,


00:55:06.291 --> 00:55:08.418
because the building
as such indicate


00:55:08.501 --> 00:55:09.377
that there is no shield.


00:55:09.461 --> 00:55:11.379
Walter Patterson, if that is so,


00:55:11.463 --> 00:55:12.839
what is the danger to life?


00:55:12.923 --> 00:55:16.259
Well, obviously if radioactivity
got as far as Scandinavia,


00:55:16.343 --> 00:55:18.762
how long, however
long it took to get there,


00:55:18.845 --> 00:55:22.015
a great deal must have
escaped close to the site.


00:56:59.446 --> 00:57:02.615
What has been remarkable
is how much the Soviet people


00:57:02.699 --> 00:57:05.869
in spite of all the evidence
still seem to be believing


00:57:05.952 --> 00:57:08.872
the fairy stories the
government is still telling them.


00:57:11.458 --> 00:57:12.709
It could be argued, Martin,


00:57:12.792 --> 00:57:15.587
that only the Soviet
Union could have kept a lid


00:57:15.670 --> 00:57:18.465
on what might have been
a situation of public panic,


00:57:18.548 --> 00:57:22.218
only a totalitarian state
could have got away with it.


00:57:22.302 --> 00:57:24.012
I'm not sure they
have got away with it.


01:32:07.051 --> 01:32:08.695
<i>Sunday's outbreak of fighting</i>


01:32:08.719 --> 01:32:11.097
<i>is only the latest in
four months of violence</i>


01:32:11.180 --> 01:32:13.349
<i>in the tiny Caucasian
republic of Georgia.</i>


01:32:13.433 --> 01:32:15.893
<i>Spasms of
unrest in the Soviet Union</i>


01:32:15.977 --> 01:32:17.562
<i>were felt in the
southern republics</i>


01:32:17.645 --> 01:32:19.939
<i>of Azerbaijan and Armenia.</i>


01:32:36.706 --> 01:32:39.333
<i>The rebellion
against Communist authority</i>


01:32:39.417 --> 01:32:40.793
<i>has now spread</i>


01:32:40.877 --> 01:32:43.296
<i>and there's almost certainly
more trouble to come.</i>


01:32:43.379 --> 01:32:44.982
<i>The decision to send in troops</i>


01:32:45.006 --> 01:32:46.883
<i>was taken by
President Gorbachev.</i>

